---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import ConfidenceInterval from '@/components/tools/calc2/ConfidenceInterval.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="confidence-interval-calculator">
  <ConfidenceInterval client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the confidence interval calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The confidence interval calculator puts a margin of error on each side of a sample mean or a sample
      proportion. The margin is a critical value times the standard error.
    </p>
    <pre><code>mean, σ known       x̄ ± z × σ ÷ √n
mean, s from data   x̄ ± t(n − 1) × s ÷ √n
proportion, Wald    p̂ ± z × √(p̂(1 − p̂) ÷ n)
proportion, Wilson  (p̂ + z²/2n ± z √(p̂(1 − p̂)/n + z²/4n²)) ÷ (1 + z²/n)</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      36 measurements have a mean of 50 and a standard deviation of 8. The standard error is 8 ÷ √36 =
      1.333. For 95% with 35 degrees of freedom, t = 2.0301, so the margin is 2.0301 × 1.333 = 2.707 and
      the interval is 47.29 to 52.71.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a proportion, 420 yes answers out of 1,000 give p̂ = 0.42. The Wilson 95% interval is 38.98% to
      45.08%.
    </p>

    <h2>What 95% means</h2>
    <p>
      The method catches the true value in 95% of samples. The simulation shows this: each row is the
      interval from a new random sample, and about 1 row in 20 misses the dashed line. A single interval
      either contains the true value or it does not.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The sample must be random. A biased sample gives a narrow interval around the wrong value.</li>
      <li>The t interval assumes roughly normal data; with 30 or more values it is robust to moderate skew.</li>
      <li>The Wald interval is poor when p̂ is near 0% or 100% or the sample is small. Wilson is better there.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
